---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-for-athletes`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D for Athletes',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D for Athletes', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D for Athletes: Performance, Stress Fractures, and Indoor-Sport Deficiency"
  description="Why indoor athletes and dark-skinned athletes at higher latitudes run consistently deficient, effects on strength and VO2max, and how vitamin D affects stress fracture risk."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-for-athletes", label: "Vitamin D for Athletes" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D for Athletes</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Skeletal muscle carries vitamin D receptors, and vitamin D affects fast-twitch
      fibre function, calcium handling, and post-exercise recovery. Deficiency is
      surprisingly common in elite athletes — particularly those in indoor sports
      (gymnastics, basketball, ice hockey, dance) and those with darker skin training
      at high latitudes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Deficiency prevalence</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Screening studies of Division I US college athletes report 30–70% deficient
      (&lt; 30 ng/mL) depending on sport and latitude. NBA players (indoor sport, mostly
      Black athletes) had 32% severely deficient (&lt; 20 ng/mL) and 47% insufficient
      (20–32 ng/mL) in a 2015 study. European football (soccer) squads show similar
      winter deficiency rates.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Performance evidence</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Muscle strength</strong> — meta-analyses show correcting deficiency modestly improves grip strength, jump height, and sprint performance. Effect largest in most-deficient athletes.</li>
      <li><strong>VO2max</strong> — mixed evidence; small trials suggest improved aerobic capacity when starting deficient.</li>
      <li><strong>Recovery</strong> — vitamin D reduces exercise-induced muscle damage markers (CK, myoglobin) in some studies.</li>
      <li><strong>Sufficient athletes</strong> — supplementation beyond 30–40 ng/mL doesn't enhance performance further. There's no ergogenic effect in already-replete athletes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stress fractures</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A landmark 2008 study of female Navy recruits found 800 IU vitamin D + 2,000 mg
      calcium daily reduced stress fracture incidence by 20% during 8 weeks of basic
      training. Similar signals exist in ballet dancers and runners. Screen and correct
      any athlete presenting with stress fracture.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical protocol</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Test 25(OH)D early season and mid-winter for all serious athletes at latitudes above 35°.</li>
      <li>Target 40–50 ng/mL (100–125 nmol/L) — the athletic target is often higher than the general-population sufficiency threshold.</li>
      <li>Deficient athletes: 4,000–5,000 IU/day cholecalciferol for 8–12 weeks, then re-test and titrate to 2,000 IU/day maintenance.</li>
      <li>Take with dietary fat (breakfast or main meal) for optimal absorption.</li>
      <li>Vitamin D is not on any anti-doping banned list — regular supplementation is compatible with WADA rules.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Persistent bone pain in a training athlete is a
      stress fracture until proven otherwise — get imaging, not just a supplement bottle.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-for-athletes" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
